   124	## Decision rules
   125	- ARM 1 alone is a measurement: no ship decision follows from it. It sets the baseline for arm 2 and is cited in the dec 061 (i)
   126	  v004 cap discussion. Arm 1 is INVALID (env problem, not a quality result) iff H rows >= 5 of 33, whatever the pass count.
   127	- ENV CONTROL (pre-registered follow-up, not gating the runs): a gold+null pass of the 33 dev ids through this exact kernel
   128	  verification path (CPU kernel, no GPU quota; needs a small gold-patch mode, codex-reviewed first). Until it exists, arm 1 is
   129	  reported with the caveat "env agreement with the local gold calibration unmeasured in the kernel path". When it exists:
   130	  ids whose kernel-path gold fails or null passes are listed and a sensitivity pass rate excludes them; the primary is unchanged.
   131	- ARM 2 vs ARM 1 (paired, same 33 ids, k=1 FIXED: no second sample under this plan, whatever arm 2 shows): per-task discordant
   132	  counts on-only / off-only, exact two-sided sign test. Any later k>1 needs a new pre-registration with a fixed n and seeds, fixed
   133	  before results, and a task-level analysis (per-task mean over samples, sign test on per-task differences).
   134	  - ON-ADOPT: on-only > off-only, p < 0.2, AND ON timeouts <= OFF timeouts + 2, AND the cap-3.5 timing check passes.
   135	  - ON-BETTER-VETOED: on-only > off-only with p < 0.2 but the timeout or timing condition fails: keep OFF for shipping; report
   136	    to the Director with the timing data as a candidate for a cap/budget follow-up (no automatic further arm).
   137	  - KEEP-OFF: off-only >= on-only.
   138	  - NO-DIFFERENCE: on ahead with p >= 0.2: keep OFF (cheaper, faster). +-3 tasks on 33 is noise.
   139	  - Cap-3.5 timing check (cap unchanged, so this checks that thinking does not break cap enforcement): every ON row has a non-null
   140	    agent_clock_seconds <= 3.5 x 60 + 10 = 220 s and finalization_seconds <= 30 s; any violation or null fails the check.
   141	- Cost note: verification adds a fresh sandbox setup per task (Container B, two dependency installs), so per-task wall is higher than
   142	  the plan-005 screens (~5.5 min/task); the smoke measures it. If arm 2 at k=1 does not fit next week's quota with other approved work,
   143	  the arm-2 schedule comes back to the Director with the measured numbers.
   144	
   145	## Repeat guard (dec 062 item 3)
   146	CPU-only design note delivered as eval/plans/007-repeat-guard.md (by 2026-11-07T18:00Z). Not built, not packed.
   147	
   148	---
   149	## Appendix Z: superseded draft (2026-11-05T22:58Z, budget lever; not scheduled)
   150	> # Plan 007: time-aware budget lever (pre-registered 2026-11-05T22:58Z; CANDIDATE, not runnable; first quality lever after report 010, dec 039 item 2)
   151	> Evidence: results/failure_taxonomy_e4b_v001_v5.json (n=8, e4b-v001, cap 1.4 min, ungraded): 4/8 hit the session timeout
   152	> without calling submit_patch (1 of these made zero edits, so its patch was empty); the finalization diff rescued a non-empty patch in 3 of the 4.
   153	> The short cap inflates the timeout rate. Re-measure the base rate at the candidate cap before trusting the size of the effect.
   154	> Lever: the bundle prompt (and a skill if needed) sets a step budget: first source edit by ~50% of the budget, run the targeted
   155	> test, then submit_patch before the cap. The agent probably can't see wall-clock time from inside a declarative ADK bundle, so the
   156	> budget is stated in LLM-call/tool-call counts derived from the measured calls-per-minute at the target cap (v5: ~9-16 LLM calls
   157	> at 1.4 min). Check what the agent can observe before building. No repo-specific content.
   158	> Hypothesis: B reduces timeout-without-submit and zero-edit outcomes and does not lower the pass rate (it may raise it slightly).
   159	> Expected effect: primary = pass rate, +0 to +3 tasks on 33 dev (small; most timeouts are already rescued by the finalization diff);
   160	> secondary = timeout-without-submit rate (expect at least halved), zero-edit/empty-patch rate, submitted-but-unverified rate (risk: an early submit
   161	> skips the test run).
   162	> Design: paired, same 33 dev ids, k=2 seeds each, same model and cap (the cap of the then-current best bundle), A = current best,
   163	> B = A + budget prompt only (byte diff limited to prompts/). Interaction with plan 005 (dec 022 item 4 empty-patch protection):
   164	> run as a 2x2 sharing arm A if compute allows (A, A+005, A+007, A+005+007); otherwise 005 first, then 007 on top of the winner.
   165	> Decision rule: adopt B if B-only > A-only with exact sign test p < 0.2 and no rise in timeouts; if p >= 0.2 but the timeout-without-
   166	> submit rate falls by >= 50% with no pass-rate loss (B-only >= A-only), adopt B as a safety change; else keep A.
   167	> Compute: local paired quality eval (GPU), not authorized; request it in the report that follows report 010. Timing re-check is only needed if
   168	> call counts change materially (not expected, since the cap is unchanged).
   169	>
   170	> ## Addendum (dec 060 item 3, 2026-11-06T04:45Z; added before any plan-007 run)
   171	> Descriptive metric, all arms: count identical consecutive tool-call repeats per task (same tool name + byte-identical args,
   172	> back to back): per-task max run length, tasks with run >= 3, total repeated calls. Evidence that motivates it:
   173	> results/shell_error_causes_31b_v005_v006.json (22 shell errors in 1 task, 21 identical repeats). Purpose: decide whether a
   174	> generic repeat guard is worth building. Descriptive only; it does not enter the decision rule.

